   "source": [
    "# Quiz: Logistic regression to predict categories\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-4-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Use Logistic Regression to predict</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>whether an apple is ripe or not</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Logistic regression predicts binary categories, with two answers like ripe or not ripe.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>how many tickets can be sold in a month</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Logistic regression predicts a binary category, like yes or no, not a number like a ticket count.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>what color the sky will turn tomorrow at 6 PM</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. This lesson uses logistic regression for binary categories, with two answers like yes or no. Sky color can have many answers.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-4-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Types of Logistic Regression include</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>multinomial and cardinal</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The other types named are multinomial and ordinal. Cardinal is not one of them.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>multinomial and ordinal</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Multinomial uses several categories, like orange, white and striped. Ordinal uses ordered categories, like pumpkin sizes.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>principal and ordinal</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The other types named are multinomial and ordinal. Principal is not one of them.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-4-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Your data has weak correlations. The best type of Regression to use is:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Logistic</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. With logistic regression, the variables don't have to correlate, so it works for data with weak correlations.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Linear</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Linear regression works better when variables are more correlated, not weakly correlated.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Cardinal</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. For data with weak correlations, use logistic regression, where the variables don't have to correlate.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-4-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Seaborn is a type of</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>data visualization library</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Seaborn is a library for data visualization, built on Matplotlib.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>mapping library</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Seaborn is used to visualize data, like the plots of pumpkin color and variety.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>mathematical library</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Seaborn is a library for data visualization. It offers neat ways to plot your data.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-4-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A confusion matrix is also known as a:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>error matrix</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Its other name is error matrix. It is a table of a model's true and false positives and negatives.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>truth matrix</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The other name for a confusion matrix is an error matrix, not a truth matrix.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>accuracy matrix</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A confusion matrix helps gauge accuracy, but its other name is error matrix.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-2-4-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A good model will have:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a large number of false positives and true negatives in its confusion matrix</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. True negatives are good, but a better model has fewer false positives, not more.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a large number of true positives and true negatives in its confusion matrix</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A better model has more true positives and true negatives, and fewer false positives and false negatives.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a large number of true positives and false negatives in its confusion matrix</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. True positives are good, but a better model has fewer false negatives, not more.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
